    "raw": "{\"ruleId\":\"item-d25238530427\",\"class\":\"partial\",\"constraints\":[{\"id\":\"ts-schnorr-sign-weak-auxrand\",\"type\":\"forbiddenPattern\",\"pattern\":\"schnorr\\\\.sign\\\\(.*(Math\\\\.random\\\\(|Date\\\\.now\\\\(|performance\\\\.now\\\\()\",\"path\":\".floor/added/**/*.ts\"},{\"id\":\"py-schnorr-sign-weak-auxrand\",\"type\":\"forbiddenPattern\",\"pattern\":\"schnorr_sign\\\\(.*(random\\\\.random\\\\(|random\\\\.randint\\\\(|random\\\\.getrandbits\\\\(|time\\\\.time\\\\()\",\"path\":\".floor/added/**/*.py\"}],\"coverage\":\"Only one flagrant form, judged on a single line. It flags added lines in .ts files where a schnorr.sign( call takes Math.random(), Date.now() or performance.now() in its arguments. It flags added lines in .py files where a schnorr_sign( call takes random.random/randint/getrandbits or time.time in its arguments. In both cases the call feeds a predictable value directly into BIP-340 signing as the aux rand or nonce.\",\"residual\":\"Apart from a Schnorr sign call written on one line with Math.random/Date.now/performance.now (TS) or Python random/time arguments, does the hunk derive a Schnorr signing nonce or aux rand from a weak or predictable source, from the message alone, from a counter that can repeat, or from a deterministic construction (e.g. RFC6979) shared with another scheme under the same key?\",\"probes\":{\"violating\":[{\"patch\":\"diff --git a/src/crypto/sign.ts b/src/crypto/sign.ts\\nnew file mode 100644\\n--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/src/crypto/sign.ts\\n@@ -0,0 +1,1 @@\\n+export const signIt = (msg: Uint8Array, priv: Uint8Array) => schnorr.sign(msg, priv, Math.random());\\n\"},{\"patch\":\"diff --git a/lib/sign.py b/lib/sign.py\\nnew file mode 100644\\n--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/lib/sign.py\\n@@ -0,0 +1,1 @@\\n+sig = schnorr_sign(msg, seckey, random.getrandbits(256).to_bytes(32, 'big'))\\n\"}],\"compliant\":{\"patch\":\"diff --git a/src/crypto/sign.ts b/src/crypto/sign.ts\\nnew file mode 100644\\n--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/src/crypto/sign.ts\\n@@ -0,0 +1,1 @@\\n+export const signIt = (msg: Uint8Array, priv: Uint8Array) => schnorr.sign(msg, priv, randomBytes(32));\\n\"}},\"rationale\":\"A weak source written directly into a Schnorr sign call is mechanically detectable on one line. Other cases need judgement: a nonce derived across several lines, from the message alone, from a counter that can repeat, or reused across schemes such as RFC6979. Deciding those means tracing data flow and reading intent, which per-line patterns cannot do. Coverage is limited to the TS and Python files scoped by the constraints.\"}",
